      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7e4dff7e-1169-50fc-82d1-646226fec189",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Vinko Knežević, Meinhard II\n- Places: County of Tyrol, Graz, Marengo\n- Organizations: Habsburg Monarchy, Austrian Empire, Austria-Hungary, Holy Roman Empire\n- Roles: Croatian nobleman, general, ruler\n\nFacts:\n- The County of Tyrol was established about 1140 as a state of the Holy Roman Empire.\n- From 1258, Meinhard II and his younger brother Albert ruled the County of Gorizia and the County of Tyrol together.\n- In 1271, Meinhard II divided the brothers' heritage with Albert and became the sole ruler of Tyrol.\n- Vinko Knežević was born on 30 November 1755 and was a Croatian nobleman and general in the Habsburg Monarchy imperial army service.\n- In 1804, the Princely County of Tyrol was unified with the secularised Prince-Bishoprics of Trent and Brixen and became a crown land of the Austrian Empire.\n- In 1805, Vinko Knežević led Austrian Empire troops in the Tyrol.\n- In 1809, Vinko Knežević led Austrian Empire troops at Graz.\n- From 1867, Tyrol was a Cisleithanian crown land of Austria-Hungary.\n- Vinko Knežević died on 11 March 1832.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "48ce7e5b-74e3-5859-976b-c872cb3edc09",
        "text": "Page: Vinko Knežević. Sentence: Vinko Knežević or Vincent Knesevich de Szent-Helena (30 November 1755 – 11 March 1832) was a Croatian nobleman and general in the Habsburg Monarchy imperial army service.\nPage: Vinko Knežević. Sentence: He commanded an infantry brigade at Marengo the following year and led Austrian Empire troops in the Tyrol in 1805 and at Graz in 1809.\nPage: County of Tyrol. Sentence: The Princely County of Tyrol, until 1493: County of Tyrol, was a State of the Holy Roman Empire established about 1140.\nPage: County of Tyrol. Sentence: In 1804 the Princely County of Tyrol, unified with the secularised Prince-Bishoprics of Trent and Brixen, became a crown land of the Austrian Empire in 1804 and from 1867 a Cisleithanian crown land of Austria-Hungary.\nPage: Meinhard, Duke of Carinthia. Sentence: Meinhard II (c. 1238 – 1 November 1295), a member of the House of Gorizia (\"Meinhardiner\"), ruled the County of Gorizia (as Meinhard IV) and the County of Tyrol together with his younger brother Albert from 1258, until in 1271 they divided their heritage and Meinhard became sole ruler of Tyrol.",
